I see `double x, y = 0.0;` - `x` is uninitialized! But `atot(e.constant.c_str(), *this, &x)` is called which should initialize `x`. Let me check if `atot` can fail without initializing `x`. Looking at `atot`:

```cpp
template<typename T> CheckedError atot(const char *s, Parser &parser, T *val) {
  auto done = atot_scalar(s, val, bool_constant<is_floating_point<T>::value>());
  if (done) return NoError();
  ...
}
```

And `atot_scalar` for floating point:
```cpp
template<typename T>
bool atot_scalar(const char *s, T *val, bool_constant<true>) {
  if (false == StringToNumber(s, val)) return false;
  *val = (*val != *val) ? std::fabs(*val) : *val;
  return true;
}
```

And `StringToNumber` for double:
```cpp
template<> inline bool StringToNumber(const char *s, double *val) {
  return StringToFloatImpl(val, s);
}
```

And `StringToFloatImpl`:
```cpp
template<typename T>
inline bool StringToFloatImpl(T *val, const char *const str) {
  FLATBUFFERS_ASSERT(str && val);
  auto end = str;
  strtoval_impl(val, str, const_cast<char **>(&end));
  auto done = (end != str) && (*end == '\0');
  if (!done) *val = 0;
  return done;
}
```

This always sets `*val`. So `x` is always initialized.

